SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

ECHO === SQLSEL INSERT vs AN INTERVENING APPEND: what does COMMIT do? ===
ECHO
ECHO THE QUESTION. sqlsel_statement.cpp:3859 reserves a recno for a buffered
ECHO INSERT as recCount64() + 1, then bumps past any recno already staged as
ECHO CHANGE_INSERT. cmd_commit.cpp:363 appends a NEW record only when
ECHO   rn == A.recCount64() + 1
ECHO and otherwise falls to an else that does gotoRec64(rn) and writes the
ECHO staged fields into whatever record is already there. If the table GROWS
ECHO between the INSERT and the COMMIT, the reservation goes stale and the
ECHO else branch is what runs.
ECHO
ECHO THIS IS ONE PROCESS ON PURPOSE. APPEND is immediate and unbuffered, so a
ECHO single process can grow the table inside the transaction window. That
ECHO measures the COMMIT PATH, which is the same code two processes reach.
ECHO
ECHO v2, after the v1 run. v1 tried to use the legacy bare INSERT verb as a
ECHO second interloper and it never fired -- shell_api.cpp:364 ALIASES INSERT
ECHO to SQLSEL whenever SET MODE SQL is in force, so v1's ARM C and ARM D got
ECHO "INSERT requires INTO <table>" and quietly became two more copies of the
ECHO control. ARM C is now the arm that PROVES that aliasing instead of
ECHO tripping over it, and ARM D uses APPEND BLANK like ARM B.
ECHO
ECHO NO ECHO LINE BELOW MAY END IN A SEMICOLON. v1 had two that did, and the
ECHO parser read the trailing ";" as a separator and swallowed the newline,
ECHO so the following ECHO printed as literal text inside the previous line.
ECHO
ECHO Reads are SMARTLIST, not LIST. OWNER RULING 2026-09-19.

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM 0 -- does the bare INSERT verb honour TABLE ON at all?
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO NATIVE mode, so this really is cmd_sql_insert.cpp and not the alias.
ECHO
ECHO THIS ARM ONCE ASSERTED THE DEFECT AND NOW ASSERTS THE REPAIR. Until
ECHO 2026-09-20 the bare verb ignored TABLE ON entirely: B0/BAREINS was on
ECHO disk before any COMMIT and survived a ROLLBACK. Same defect and same
ECHO repair as MULTIREP under AIF-151. An arm that only ever described a
ECHO defect cannot tell you the day it stops being one.
ECHO
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO ARM 0a -- TABLE ON, then ROLLBACK. The row must NOT survive.
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
TABLE ON
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("B0","BAREINS")
ECHO   FIXED  -> count 0. The row is STAGED and the DBF is untouched.
ECHO   BROKEN -> count 1 with B0 on disk before any COMMIT ran.
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10
ROLLBACK
ECHO after ROLLBACK:
ECHO   FIXED  -> count 0 and no B0 anywhere. ROLLBACK DISCARDED IT.
ECHO   BROKEN -> B0 survives, which is what it did before this date.
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10
TABLE OFF

ECHO
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO ARM 0b -- TABLE ON, then COMMIT. Both forms, both must land.
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO Discarding on ROLLBACK proves only half of it. A verb that staged and
ECHO then lost the row at COMMIT would pass 0a and be far worse than the
ECHO defect it replaced.
ECHO
ECHO BOTH SYNTAX FORMS RUN HERE ON PURPOSE. cmd_sql_insert.cpp had the
ECHO append-and-write sequence written out TWICE, once per form, and only a
ECHO probe that exercises both can say the fork was applied to both.
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
TABLE ON
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("K1","TUPLEFORM")
INSERT ID="K2", TAG="ASSIGNFORM"
ECHO staged -- count must still be 0:
COUNT
COMMIT
ECHO after COMMIT:
ECHO   FIXED  -> count 2, record 1 = K1 / TUPLEFORM, record 2 = K2 / ASSIGNFORM
ECHO   BROKEN -> any other count, or the rows out of order. The buffer key is
ECHO             minted by next_insert_key and COMMIT walks the multimap in
ECHO             key order, so key order IS append order.
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10
TABLE OFF

ECHO
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO ARM 0c -- TABLE OFF is UNCHANGED. The control.
ECHO ------------------------------------------------------------
ECHO The whole point of a fork is that the other branch still works. With
ECHO TABLE OFF this verb still fences, appends and writes immediately, and
ECHO ROLLBACK has nothing to discard.
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("U1","UNBUFFERED")
ECHO   EXPECT count 1 IMMEDIATELY -- no COMMIT was run and none is needed.
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM A -- CONTROL. SQLSEL INSERT, nothing intervenes.
ECHO ############################################################
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S1","SEED1")
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S2","SEED2")
ECHO seeded, expect 2:
COUNT
SET MODE SQL
SQLSEL BEGIN TRANSACTION
SQLSEL INSERT INTO SQLINS (ID,TAG) VALUES ("A1","ARM_A")
ECHO staged -- count should still be 2 on disk:
COUNT
SQLSEL COMMIT
SQLSEL ROLLBACK
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO EXPECT 3 records, record 3 = A1 / ARM_A:
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM B -- APPEND BLANK intervenes. ONE staged row.
ECHO ############################################################
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S1","SEED1")
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S2","SEED2")
SET MODE SQL
SQLSEL BEGIN TRANSACTION
SQLSEL INSERT INTO SQLINS (ID,TAG) VALUES ("B1","ARM_B")
ECHO staged at recno 3. Now grow the table IMMEDIATELY, inside the window.
ECHO APPEND goes through append_support.cpp:619, which DOES take the table
ECHO lock -- but the owner token is host:pid:ms minted once per process, so
ECHO in ONE process the lock is re-entrant and this gets through. Across two
ECHO processes it would be refused, and that is the fence working.
APPEND BLANK
ECHO after APPEND, expect 3 on disk:
COUNT
SQLSEL COMMIT
SQLSEL ROLLBACK
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO
ECHO   HAZARD  -> count 3, record 3 reads B1 / ARM_B. The appended blank row
ECHO              was silently overwritten and COMMIT said nothing
ECHO   SAFE    -> count 4, record 3 blank, record 4 reads B1 / ARM_B
ECHO   REFUSED -> COMMIT printed a refusal and the buffer is still held
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM C -- the NO-LOCK door, and why it needs two processes.
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO cmd_sql_insert.cpp calls A.appendBlank() / A.writeCurrent() directly and
ECHO the string try_lock_table appears nowhere in that file. DbArea::appendBlank
ECHO (dbf_file.cpp:364) takes no lock either. So the legacy verb can grow a
ECHO table that another process has fenced with a table lock.
ECHO
ECHO THIS ARM CANNOT PROVOKE THAT IN ONE PROCESS, and says so rather than
ECHO pretending. In SQL mode shell_api.cpp:364 routes INSERT to SQLSEL, and
ECHO SET MODE NATIVE is refused while a transaction is open (cmd_set.cpp:730).
ECHO There is no in-process path to the legacy verb inside the window.
ECHO
ECHO What it DOES assert is the aliasing itself, because that aliasing is the
ECHO only thing standing between a SQL-mode session and the unlocked door.
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S1","SEED1")
SET MODE SQL
ECHO A bare INSERT in SQL mode must be REFUSED BY THE SQLSEL PARSER
ECHO ("INSERT requires INTO <table> ..."). If it instead says "1 row(s)
ECHO inserted", the alias is gone and the unlocked door is open in-process.
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("C1","ARM_C")
ECHO And SET MODE NATIVE inside an open transaction must be REFUSED.
SQLSEL BEGIN TRANSACTION
SQLSEL INSERT INTO SQLINS (ID,TAG) VALUES ("C2","ARM_C2")
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO If the line above said "MODE: NATIVE" the escape hatch is real.
SQLSEL ROLLBACK
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO expect 1 record, the seed only:
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM D -- TWO staged rows, ONE APPEND BLANK. The misalignment shape.
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO Staged recnos are 3 and 4. One interloper makes the table 3 records.
ECHO COMMIT walks the multimap in ascending recno. recno 3 finds
ECHO 3 != recCount()+1 (4) and falls to the else -> overwrites the
ECHO interloper. recno 4 then finds 4 == recCount()+1 (4) and appends
ECHO correctly. So the FIRST staged row eats the interloper and the SECOND
ECHO lands cleanly -- one row lost, applied_ok reports 2, no error anywhere.
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S1","SEED1")
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S2","SEED2")
SET MODE SQL
SQLSEL BEGIN TRANSACTION
SQLSEL INSERT INTO SQLINS (ID,TAG) VALUES ("D1","ARM_D1"),("D2","ARM_D2")
APPEND BLANK
ECHO after APPEND, expect 3 on disk:
COUNT
SQLSEL COMMIT
SQLSEL ROLLBACK
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO
ECHO   HAZARD  -> count 4, record 3 = D1 / ARM_D1 with the blank row eaten,
ECHO              record 4 = D2 / ARM_D2
ECHO   SAFE    -> count 5, record 3 blank, record 4 = D1, record 5 = D2
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO ARM E -- TWO interlopers, ONE staged row. How far does it reach?
ECHO ############################################################
ECHO Staged recno is 3. Two APPENDs make the table 4 records. At COMMIT
ECHO 3 != recCount()+1 (5), and the else branch guards only rn > recCount(),
ECHO which 3 is not -- so it overwrites record 3 and record 4 survives.
ECHO The guard is a BOUNDS CHECK, not a staleness check, and this arm is
ECHO what shows the difference.
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
CREATE X64 SQLINS (ID C(4), TAG C(12))
USE SQLINS
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S1","SEED1")
INSERT (ID,TAG) VALUES ("S2","SEED2")
SET MODE SQL
SQLSEL BEGIN TRANSACTION
SQLSEL INSERT INTO SQLINS (ID,TAG) VALUES ("E1","ARM_E")
APPEND BLANK
APPEND BLANK
ECHO after two APPENDs, expect 4 on disk:
COUNT
SQLSEL COMMIT
SQLSEL ROLLBACK
SET MODE NATIVE
ECHO
ECHO   HAZARD  -> count 4, record 3 = E1 / ARM_E, record 4 still blank
ECHO   SAFE    -> count 5, records 3 and 4 blank, record 5 = E1 / ARM_E
COUNT
SMARTLIST 10

ECHO
ECHO --- teardown ---
SET MODE NATIVE
USE
ERASE SQLINS CONFIRM
ECHO === probe done ===
